  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 AM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Another morning of patchy fog is expected, although \ncoverage and intensity is not expected to be as great today as \nyesterday. Friday will likely be the warmest out of the next seven \ndays.\n\nSome patchy fog is expected to develop again. However, compared to \nyesterday, when an area of low clouds moved from the Coastal Plain \ninto the central portion of the state along with reduced \nvisibilities, there have been passing mid level clouds through much \nof the night. These mid level clouds should help to limit the \noverall coverage of fog that occurs in the next few hours. With \ndrier air moving into the region, any clouds/fog that do develop \naround sunrise should clear out more quickly, with mostly sunny \nskies by the afternoon.\n\nNormal high temperatures are around 70 degrees this time of year, \nand highs will run slightly above normal, with all locations \nexpected to be in the 70s. Friday appears likely to be the warmest \nday out of the next seven, with highs between 78 and 83 degrees. A \ncold front moving through Friday night should bring highs back into \nthe 60s and 70s for Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Very light, non-accumulating rain, is possible Sun \nnight into Mon, but better chance for accumulating rainfall remains \nin the mid/late next week period.\n\nThe chance for measurable rainfall continues to decrease for the Sun \nnight into Mon timeframe. Although, confidence is medium-high on \nincreasing widespread cloud cover with some very light rain possible \nfalling out of mid-level cloud bases. Moisture associated with \nTropical Cyclone Vance is expected to lift through southwest Mexico \nand into the Four Corners and Southern Plains region Fri before \nshifting eastward into the Southeast Sat into Sun and perhaps \nCarolinas Sun night into Mon. The mid/upper-lvl wave, which will be \nmore pronounced as it shifts through the Southern Plains, will shear \nout substantially as it approaches the Southeast and Carolinas, with \nmostly convectively modified waves providing deeper forcing \naccompanying the sharp, fast-moving upper trough. Any falling precip \nwill have to overcome drier mid/low-lvl air from a reinforcing dry \ncold front that will provide renewed high pressure persisting over \nthe Mid-Atlantic into the Carolinas this weekend. The GFS and its \nensemble suite continues to come in line with most other guidance as \nit shunts the better QPF signal farther south with only light \naccumulations in NC Sun night into Tues night.\n\nThe better signal for measurable rainfall continues to be sometime \nwithin the Wed into Fri night timeframe as a strong mid/upper low \nshifts from the West Coast on Sun into the Central Plains and MS \nValley into the mid-week timeframe. How and when this feature will \ndevelop is understandably uncertain as the driving features will not \nphase until this weekend.",
